# Crossfire Co-Streaming Guidelines
 
*Last updated: October 28, 2026*
 
**Contents:** [Overview](#overview) · [How to get approved](#how-to-get-approved) · [Broadcast rules](#broadcast-rules) · [Sponsors](#sponsors) · [Conduct, chat and guests](#conduct-chat-and-guests) · [VODs and clips](#vods-and-clips) · [Platform co-stream tools](#platform-co-stream-tools) · [Stream stats](#stream-stats) · [Enforcement](#enforcement)
 
## Overview
 
Crossfire welcomes co-streams, but every co-streamer must be approved first. Streaming the official Crossfire broadcast without approval is a rebroadcast of our production and will be taken down.
 
- **Co-stream (watch party):** you show the official Crossfire broadcast on your channel and add your own commentary, camera or reactions. These guidelines cover co-streams.
- **Community broadcast:** you cast a match yourself from the in-game spectator feed (CSTV) with your own production. This needs separate written approval from a Crossfire admin and is not covered by a co-stream approval.
- **Player POV streams:** players follow the player streaming rules in the Crossfire rulebook, not these guidelines.
## How to get approved
 
Apply in the Crossfire Discord before you co-stream.
 
1. Post in the co-stream application channel, or open a ticket, with:
   - Your channel link and platform (Twitch, YouTube, Kick or another platform)
   - The matches or match days you want to co-stream
   - Your channel sponsors, if you have any
2. An admin reviews your channel and replies in Discord.
3. Once approved, you get the **Verified Co-Streamer** role. Do not go live with Crossfire content before you have it.
4. Approval is personal to you and your channel. It cannot be shared or transferred.
5. Any regular co-hosts must also apply and be approved.
Approval can cover a single match day or the whole season, as stated in your approval message.
 
## Broadcast rules
 
- **Add commentary.** You must add live commentary, a camera or reactions. Showing the feed with no added content is a rebroadcast, not a co-stream.
- **Official feed only.** Use the official Crossfire broadcast as provided. Do not re-encode it from another co-streamer's channel.
- **Keep it visible and audible.** The Crossfire broadcast must stay on screen with its audio on for the full broadcast, including breaks, sponsor segments, interviews and the analyst desk. Your camera and overlay may not cover the scoreboard, team names, sponsor logos or broadcast graphics.
- **Stay on the event.** Do not switch to other games, other streams or Just Chatting while the Crossfire broadcast is live.
- **Stream title.** Your title must start with: `Crossfire – Team A vs Team B (Co-stream)`. Use the current season name if one is announced.
- **No in-game spectating.** Co-streamers may not join the match server or use CSTV for their co-stream. That is a community broadcast and needs separate approval (see Overview).
- **Platform rules apply.** Follow the terms of service of the platform you stream on.
## Sponsors
 
Crossfire's sponsors pay for the event's visibility, so their placements must reach your viewers exactly as broadcast.
 
- Do not cover, crop, blur, mute, skip or talk over sponsor logos, ad reads or sponsored segments.
- Do not mock or disparage Crossfire or its sponsors.
- Platform-native ads (Twitch, YouTube or Kick ads) and your normal subs, bits and donations are allowed.
- Your own channel sponsors may appear on screen only if they:
  - Do not compete with a Crossfire sponsor
  - Are not in a prohibited category
  - Do not cover any part of the Crossfire broadcast
**Prohibited sponsor categories during a Crossfire co-stream:**
 
- Gambling, sports betting and esports betting
- Skin gambling, case opening, skin trading and skin marketplace sites, or any other third-party site that uses Valve's IP (such as CS2 skins, items or branding)
- Alcohol, tobacco, vaping and drugs
- Adult content
- Political advertising
If you are unsure whether a sponsor is allowed, ask an admin before you go live.
 
## Conduct, chat and guests
 
- **You represent the event while you co-stream it.** This includes your Discord and social posts about Crossfire.
- **Moderate your chat.** You are responsible for removing hate speech, harassment and spam aimed at players, casters or admins.
- **No harassment of players.** Criticism of gameplay and decisions is fine. Personal attacks, slurs or targeting of players are not.
- **Guests follow these rules too.** Regular co-hosts must apply and be approved before they appear. Anyone else on your stream for more than a few minutes must be named to an admin in advance. You are responsible for what they say and do.
- **No players in active matches.** A player whose team is still playing that match day may not appear on your co-stream. This prevents ghosting.
- **No claims of being official.** Do not call your stream "official" or imply you represent Crossfire, its sponsors, Valve or FACEIT.
- **No banned players.** Players banned by Crossfire, FACEIT or Valve may not appear on your stream.
## VODs and clips
 
- Your co-stream VOD may stay on your channel's VOD archive.
- Do not upload the full co-stream to YouTube or another platform until 7 days after the match.
- Short clips under 60 seconds can be posted at any time if they credit Crossfire.
- By co-streaming, you allow Crossfire to use clips of your co-stream, including your voice and likeness, in Crossfire promotion and highlight videos. Crossfire will not use them in paid ads without asking you.
## Platform co-stream tools
 
- **Use your platform's built-in co-stream feature when it has one.** This applies to Twitch, Kick, YouTube or any other platform. After approval, Crossfire adds you to the official channel's co-streamer list on that platform. Start your co-stream through that feature rather than capturing the feed yourself, so your viewers count toward the event's total reach.
- **Platform rules still apply.** Restreaming Crossfire without permission breaks the rules of Twitch, Kick, YouTube and most other platforms, and can lead to a copyright strike or takedown on your channel.
## Stream stats
 
**FILL OUT THIS FORM after every match day you co-stream:** [Crossfire co-stream stats form](https://forms.gle/6zwVr2ArfFngGQ2NA)
 
- Submit it within 24 hours of the end of the broadcast.
- Crossfire uses these numbers to report total viewership to sponsors.
- Missing the form counts as a strike 1 violation, and you need to submit it before your next approved match day.
## Enforcement
 
Crossfire uses a three-strike system for each season. Admins can skip straight to removal for serious violations.
 
| Strike | Example violation | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Wrong title, overlay covering a graphic | Warning in DM. Fix it within 10 minutes. |
| 2 | Cutting away during a sponsor segment, ignoring a warning | Co-stream approval removed for the rest of that match day |
| 3 | Any third violation in a season | Approval removed for the rest of the season |
| Immediate | Prohibited sponsor, covering a sponsor on purpose, harassment, ghosting, no commentary | Approval removed and the stream reported to its platform |
 
Streaming Crossfire's broadcast without approval is treated as a rebroadcast. Crossfire will ask the streamer to stop, and file a copyright takedown with the platform if they do not.
 
Crossfire may update these guidelines or remove any approval at any time. Questions go to an admin in Discord.
 
